      "filename": "1990-04-21 Berlin-Mitte Bahnhof Berlin-Friedrichstraße Schiffbauerdamm (Ostberlin) a.jpg",
      "title": "File:1990-04-21 Berlin-Mitte Bahnhof Berlin-Friedrichstraße Schiffbauerdamm (Ostberlin) a.jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0d/1990-04-21_Berlin-Mitte_Bahnhof_Berlin-Friedrichstra%C3%9Fe_Schiffbauerdamm_%28Ostberlin%29_a.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1990-04-21_Berlin-Mitte_Bahnhof_Berlin-Friedrichstra%C3%9Fe_Schiffbauerdamm_(Ostberlin)_a.jpg",
      "width": 3434,
      "height": 2242,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0",
      "artist": "Lothar Weber",
      "description": "On May 15, 1882, the station was fully opened as \"Centralbahnhof Friedrichstraße.\" It had two platforms, each with two directional tracks, and a large arched platform hall above trusses of varying spans that arched over the curve of the line. Due to rapidly increasing traffic, the station was gradually demolished and rebuilt in an expanded form while still in operation from 1914 onward. A new, slightly raised platform was built on the north side for the suburban tracks. The steel, side-glazed double hall was built between 1919 and 1925. During this time, construction was already underway underground, and on January 30, 1923, the subway station for the first wide-gauge line was completed. In the early 1930s, the station again became a construction site when the north-south tunnel of the Berlin S-Bahn was excavated beneath the western end of the platform halls and opened in July 1936.",
